<p>A puzzle by National Security Agency applied research mathematician Nicholas R., from the agency&#8217;s <a href="https://www.nsa.gov/Press-Room/News-Highlights/Article/Article/1627332/october-puzzle-periodical-cyberchance/">October 2016 Puzzle Periodical</a>:</p>
<p>Eddie, Layne, Kurt, and Chris are locked in a heated game of CyberChance which only one of them will win. Their fans are on the edge of their seats: everyone knows that in CyberChance, things can change at any moment. One fan, who prefers Kurt or Chris, is feeling worried since there is only a 4-in-10 chance that one of them will win. An Eddie fan brags that Eddie is twice as likely as Layne to come out on top. Another Eddie fan concurs, and adds the following observation: Chris is Eddie&#8217;s main competition. He figures that, given that Chris doesn&#8217;t win, Eddie has a 4-in-7 chance of winning. A Kurt fan has been quietly standing in the corner, listening to all the other fans, and wonders: What chance does Kurt have of winning CyberChance?</p>

